GAD.reg("lesson","cma.oh.ads",{"id":"cma.oh.ads","title":"Administration, selling, distribution and R&D overheads","md":"## In plain words\n\n### Who pays for the boardroom chai?\n\nTapri's head office in Indore costs lakhs every month: the board, finance, legal, the audit fee. The sales team, the Diwali advertising and the dealers' commission cost more. The delivery vans and godowns cost more again. None of it fries a samosa.\n\n> **Kesar:** The factory makes the samosas. Why should a samosa pay for the board's chai?\n> **Ritu:** Because without the board there is no factory, no price list and no samosa.\n> **Kesar:** Then add 20% to every pack and be done. Or put it all in miscellaneous.\n> **Ritu:** The first is one of ICAI's three methods. The second is not.\n\n**Administration overheads** are the costs of general management and of secretarial, accounting and administrative services, which cannot be related directly to production, marketing, research or development. **Selling overheads** are incurred to promote marketing and sales (salesmen, advertising), and **distribution overheads** to deliver and despatch goods sold (vans, transit insurance, godowns). They sit below works cost in the [[cma.sheet.mfg|cost sheet]], and each has its own question: how much of it each product should carry, and how to keep it from growing.\n\nAdministration costs are mostly fixed and arise from management's policy; selling and distribution costs depend on markets, competitors and customers. Both are hard to tie to a unit and hard to control. ICAI's answers are a choice of methods and bases for absorbing them, and three tools for control: comparison with the past, budgets and standards. **Research and development** costs go wherever the research points: to production, to administration, to selling, to one product, or to Costing P&L if the research fails.\n\n::: analogy The school principal's salary\nA school's teachers are its production cost. Who bears the principal's salary? The school can split it between the classrooms and the admissions office (apportion between production and sales), treat it as a school-wide expense charged to no class (Costing P&L), or add it to each class as a separate charge on its fees or its number of students (a separate addition to cost). The admission brochures are selling costs, and the school bus is distribution.\n:::\n\n::: key\nAdministration overheads are apportioned between production and sales, charged to Costing P&L, or added to cost as a separate charge on a base such as works cost; selling and distribution overheads are best absorbed as an estimated amount per unit (fixed costs by benefit, plus variable costs). Both are controlled by comparison, budgets and standards. R&D follows the purpose of the research.\n:::\n\n## How it works\n\n### Administration overheads\n\n::: definition Administrative overhead\n\"The sum of those costs of general management and of secretarial accounting and administrative services, which cannot be directly related to the production, marketing, research or development functions of the enterprise.\" (CIMA Terminology, SM 4.46)\n:::\n\nThey are the expenses of formulating policy, directing the organisation and controlling its operations, and are collected and classified like factory overheads. ICAI gives three methods of accounting for them, in this order (SM 4.47–4.48):\n\n::: diagram Three ways to account for administrative overheads\n{\"type\": \"tree\", \"gapX\": 22, \"root\": {\"label\": \"Administrative\\noverheads\", \"color\": \"mute\", \"children\": [\n {\"label\": \"(a) Apportion to\\nproduction and\\nsales departments\", \"color\": \"a\"},\n {\"label\": \"(b) Charge to\\nCosting P&L\", \"color\": \"c\"},\n {\"label\": \"(c) Separate\\naddition to cost of\\nproduction or sales\", \"color\": \"g\"}]},\n \"caption\": \"Under (a) they merge into production and selling and distribution overheads; under (b) they never reach product cost; under (c) they stay a separate function with their own rate.\"}\n:::\n\n| Method (SM 4.47–4.48) | Why | Disadvantages, or bases |\n|---|---|---|\n| **(a) Apportion between production and sales departments** | Incurred for the benefit of both, so each bears a share; they lose their identity and merge with production and selling and distribution overheads | (1) Suitable bases are difficult to find; (2) a lot of clerical work; (3) not justified to apportion only over production and sales when equally important departments, like finance, exist |\n| **(b) Charge to Costing Profit and Loss Account** | They concern policy formulation, not production or selling directly; a suitable basis is difficult to find; they are fixed costs | (1) Cost of products is understated; (2) excluding them from product cost is against sound accounting principle |\n| **(c) A separate addition to cost of production or sales** | Administration is a separate function like production and sales, charged to each job or product with the cost of the other functions | Bases used: (i) works cost; (ii) sales value or quantity; (iii) gross profit on sales; (iv) quantity produced; (v) conversion cost, etc. |\n\n**Control.** Mostly fixed, arising from management policy and generally non-controllable, they should still not be allowed to grow disproportionately. Three methods (SM 4.48–4.49):\n\n1. **Classification and analysis by administrative departments according to their functions, compared with the results accomplished.** Each department's expenses are collected under standing orders and compared with the previous period in relation to accomplishment. This gives limited control when activity is not constant, so absorption rates may also be compared from period to period: the extent of over- or under-absorption reveals efficiency. A service department's cost may be compared with the price of the same service from outside, to decide whether to continue it or entrust the work to outsiders.\n2. **Control through budgets**: monthly or annual administration budgets for each department, compared with actuals; variances analysed and responsibility assigned.\n3. **Control through standards**: standards of performance for each administrative activity, compared with actual performance.\n\n### Selling and distribution overheads\n\nSelling overheads are the expenses of promoting the marketing and sales of different products; distribution expenses relate to delivery and despatch of goods sold. Many concerns group them as one class, **selling and distribution overheads**, collected through standing orders (SM 4.53). The usual absorption bases, with ICAI's comments (SM 4.53–4.54):\n\n| Basis | ICAI's comment |\n|---|---|\n| (a) Sales value of goods | Ordinarily ==the most logical basis==: there is some connection between sales and the expenses incurred to achieve them |\n| (b) Cost of goods sold | Not as satisfactory: it may have no direct relationship with selling and distribution cost |\n| (c) Gross profit on sales | Gives a larger share to goods with a large margin: follows the principle of \"ability to pay\"; may not reflect costs or efforts incurred |\n| (d) Number of orders or units sold | One of the usual bases |\n\n**The best method, an estimated amount per unit.** Separate fixed expenses from variable ones. Apportion the fixed expenses according to the benefit each product derives, then divide by the units sold or likely to be sold; add the variable expenses per unit, worked out for each product: packaging, freight outwards, insurance in transit, commission to salesmen, rebate to customers (SM 4.54). ICAI's bases for the fixed ones:\n\n| Fixed expense | Basis of apportionment (SM 4.54) |\n|---|---|\n| Salaries of the sales department and of salesmen | Estimated time devoted to the sale of each product |\n| Advertisement | Actual amount for each product; common expenses (an exhibition) on each product's advertisement expenditure |\n| Show room expenses | Average space occupied by each product |\n| Rent of finished goods godowns; expenses on own delivery vans | Average quantities delivered during a period |\n| No suitable basis | Proportion of sales of the products |\n\n**Control is difficult**, for three reasons (SM 4.55):\n\n1. Their incidence depends mainly on **external factors** beyond management's control: distance of the market, extent and nature of competition, terms of sale.\n2. They depend on **customers' behaviour**, likes, dislikes and tastes, so controlling them may lose customers.\n3. Being **policy costs**, they are not amenable to control.\n\nIn spite of this, three methods may be used: **(a) comparison with past performance** (the expenses, or the expenses as a percentage of sales, against the previous period; suitable for small concerns); **(b) budgetary control** (a budget split into fixed and variable, if necessary a [[cma.bud.flexible|flexible budget]] for different levels of sales, actuals compared and variances acted on); **(c) standard costing** (standards for salesmen, territories, products, set in relation to the standard sales volume; actuals compared and variances enquired into).\n\n### Research and development expenses\n\n::: definition Research expenses; development expenses\nResearch expenses: \"the expenses of searching for new or improved products, new application of materials, or new or improved methods.\" Development expenses: \"the expenses of the process which begins with the implementation of the decision to produce a new or improved product.\" (Terminology, SM 4.62)\n:::\n\n::: diagram Where an R&D expense goes (SM 4.62–4.63)\n{\"type\": \"nest\", \"w\": 430, \"root\": {\"title\": \"Research and development expenses\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"mute\", \"children\": [\n {\"title\": \"Cost of production, a separate item\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"a\", \"lines\": [\"research in the methods of production\"]},\n {\"title\": \"The particular product\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"g\", \"lines\": [\"development costs of that product:\", \"deferred revenue expenses, recovered per unit\", \"once production is fully established\"]},\n {\"title\": \"Administration overhead\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"c\", \"lines\": [\"research relating to administration\", \"fundamental research, not for a specific product\"]},\n {\"title\": \"Selling and distribution overhead\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"d\", \"lines\": [\"market research\"]},\n {\"title\": \"General overhead\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"f\", \"lines\": [\"routine research on new or improved methods,\", \"or on improving existing products;\", \"if substantial, deferred over the benefit period\"]},\n {\"title\": \"Costing P&L, excluded from costs\", \"mono\": false, \"color\": \"bad\", \"lines\": [\"research that proves a failure\"]}]},\n \"caption\": \"Each box is a destination, and its lines the research that goes there. Blue and green: product cost; amber, orange and violet: overheads; rose: written off.\"}\n:::\n\n### Other items: what ICAI says\n\n| Item (SM 4.59–4.62) | Treatment |\n|---|---|\n| **Interest and financing charges**: interest for the use of non-equity funds and the incidental cost of arranging them (interest on borrowings, financing charges of finance leases, cash discount allowed to customers); not imputed costs | Presented in the cost statement as a **separate item of cost of sales** |\n| **Depreciation**: \"the diminution in the intrinsic value of an asset due to use and/or the lapse of time\" | Traced to the cost object to the extent economically feasible; otherwise assigned on (i) cause and effect or (ii) benefit received |\n| **Bad debts** | No unanimity. One view: excluded from cost, being financial losses. Another: part of selling and distribution overheads when they arise in the normal course of trading. Extraordinarily large bad debts are not included in cost accounts |\n| **Carriage and cartage** | On direct material: cost of direct material; on indirect material (stores): factory overheads; on finished goods: distribution overhead |\n| **Night shift allowance** | Usually caused by pressure of work beyond normal capacity: a production overhead, recovered as such (as direct wages it would make night jobs costlier than day jobs). For a specific customer's order: charged to that order. Due to abnormal circumstances: Costing P&L |\n\nPacking (primary, secondary, special and fancy), fringe benefits, removal and re-erection of machines, canteen, welfare and training expenses are tabulated in [[cma.oh.classify|classifying overheads]].\n\n## Formats and formulas\n\n*Reproduce the reverse-calculation steps and the product-wise statement from memory.*\n\n::: formula Administration and selling rates on a base\n$$\\text{Admin. rate} = \\frac{\\text{Admin. overheads}}{\\text{Works cost}} \\times 100 \\qquad \\text{S\\&D rate} = \\frac{\\text{S\\&D overheads}}{\\text{Sales value}} \\times 100$$\nWorks cost is factory cost. The base is the one the question names: works cost, cost of production, sales value, units.\n:::\n\n::: formula Estimated selling and distribution expense per unit (SM 4.54)\n$$\\text{S\\&D per unit} = \\frac{\\text{Fixed S\\&D of the product}}{\\text{Units sold}} \\qquad + \\text{Variable per unit}$$\nFixed S&D of the product: the fixed expenses apportioned to it on their bases. Units sold: sold or likely to be sold. Variable per unit: packaging, freight outwards, insurance in transit, commission, rebate.\n:::\n\n::: formula Working back from a selling price\n$$\\text{Total cost} = \\frac{\\text{Price}}{1 + p_c} \\qquad \\text{Total cost} = \\text{Price} \\times (1 - p_s)$$\n$p_c$ = profit as a fraction of cost; $p_s$ = profit as a fraction of the selling price. With factory overheads at F% of direct wages and administration at A% of factory cost: factory cost = prime cost + F × direct wages; total cost = factory cost × (1 + A).\n:::\n\n### Product-wise statement of profit (ICAI's layout, SM 4.57)\n\n| Particulars | Product A (₹) | Product B (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Sales | xxx | xxx |\n| Less: Variable costs: cost of goods sold, commission, packing, stationery | xxx | xxx |\n| **Contribution** | **xxx** | **xxx** |\n| Less: Fixed costs, each apportioned on its basis (ratio shown) | xxx | xxx |\n| **Profit or loss** | **xxx** | **xxx** |\n| **Percentage of profit or loss on sales** | **x%** | **x%** |\n\nICAI's statement also has a Total column; write it first.\n\n## Worked examples\n\n:::: example Administration overheads: works cost, conversion cost or units?\nTapri's Indore factory makes two products. Per unit and for the year:\n\n| Particulars | Chai premix (per box) | Frozen samosa (per pack) |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Direct materials (₹) | 60 | 20 |\n| Direct wages (₹) | 10 | 25 |\n| Factory overheads (₹) | 15 | 35 |\n| Units produced and sold | 2,00,000 | 1,00,000 |\n\nAdministration overheads of ₹22,00,000 (accounts, payroll, HR and the general office) are treated as a separate addition to the cost of production. Ritu must choose the base, and the factory prices at cost of production plus 20%.\n\n**Required:**\n\n(i) Calculate the administration overhead per unit of each product if it is absorbed on (a) works cost, (b) conversion cost, (c) quantity produced.\n\n(ii) Calculate the cost of production and the price of each product under each base.\n\n(iii) Advise on the base, given that most of the administration work is payroll and HR for the people in the factory and the office.\n\n::: answer Show the working\n**Working note: the three bases**\n\n| Base | Chai premix (₹) | Frozen samosa (₹) | Total (₹) | Rate |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Works cost (₹85 / ₹80 a unit) | 1,70,00,000 | 80,00,000 | 2,50,00,000 | 8.8% |\n| Conversion cost: wages + factory overheads (₹25 / ₹60 a unit) | 50,00,000 | 60,00,000 | 1,10,00,000 | 20% |\n| Quantity produced (units) | 2,00,000 | 1,00,000 | 3,00,000 | ₹7.33 a unit |\n\nRates: ₹22,00,000 ÷ ₹2,50,00,000 = 8.8%; ₹22,00,000 ÷ ₹1,10,00,000 = 20%; ₹22,00,000 ÷ 3,00,000 = ₹7.33.\n\n**(i) and (ii) Administration overhead, cost of production and price per unit**\n\n| Base | Premix: admin (₹) | Premix: cost / price (₹) | Samosa: admin (₹) | Samosa: cost / price (₹) |\n|---|---:|---|---:|---|\n| (a) Works cost, 8.8% | 7.48 | 92.48 / 110.98 | 7.04 | 87.04 / 104.45 |\n| (b) Conversion cost, 20% | 5.00 | 90.00 / 108.00 | 12.00 | 92.00 / 110.40 |\n| (c) Quantity produced | 7.33 | 92.33 / 110.80 | 7.33 | 87.33 / 104.80 |\n\nEach base recovers the full ₹22,00,000. For example, on works cost: 2,00,000 × ₹7.48 + 1,00,000 × ₹7.04 = ₹14,96,000 + ₹7,04,000.\n\n**(iii) Advice.** The Study Material lists works cost, sales value or quantity, gross profit on sales, quantity produced and conversion cost among the bases for treating administration overheads as a separate addition (SM 4.48). The base should follow what causes the overhead. Here the work is mostly payroll and HR for people, and the samosa line employs far more labour (₹25 of wages a pack against ₹10 a box). Works cost loads the material-heavy premix with administration it does not cause, because ₹60 of its ₹85 is material; a rate per unit treats a box and a pack as making the same demand. Conversion cost follows the labour and factory activity that the administration actually serves, so it is the most suitable of the three: the samosa's price rises to ₹110.40 and the premix's falls to ₹108.00.\n:::\n::::\n\n::::: example Three channels, one advertising budget\nTapri sells through its own cafés, a food-delivery app and supermarkets. For 2025-26:\n\n| Particulars | Cafés | Delivery app | Supermarkets |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Sales (₹) | 80,00,000 | 60,00,000 | 40,00,000 |\n| Cost of goods sold (₹) | 40,00,000 | 30,00,000 | 26,00,000 |\n| Orders or invoices | 2,00,000 | 75,000 | 4,000 |\n\nSelling and distribution costs:\n\n| Cost | Amount and nature |\n|---|---|\n| App platform commission | 20% of app sales (variable) |\n| Delivery packaging | ₹8 per app order (variable) |\n| Card and UPI charges | 1% of café and app sales (variable) |\n| Freight to supermarkets | ₹100 per invoice (variable) |\n| Supermarket merchandisers | ₹4,80,000 (fixed, supermarkets only) |\n| Promotions run with the app | ₹3,00,000 (fixed, app only) |\n| Brand advertising and sales office | ₹12,60,000 (fixed, common to all, apportioned on sales value) |\n\n> **Ananya:** App orders bring ₹80 each against ₹40 in the cafés. The app is our best channel.\n\n**Required:**\n\n(i) Prepare a statement of contribution and profit by channel.\n\n(ii) Calculate the contribution per ₹100 of sales and per order of each channel.\n\n(iii) An extra ₹5,00,000 of advertising is expected to add ₹25,00,000 of sales to whichever channel it is spent on, with the same cost structure. Advise where to spend it, and comment on Ananya's view.\n\n:::: answer Show the working\n**(i) Statement of profit by channel**\n\n| Particulars | Cafés (₹) | App (₹) | Supermarkets (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Sales | 80,00,000 | 60,00,000 | 40,00,000 |\n| Less: Cost of goods sold | 40,00,000 | 30,00,000 | 26,00,000 |\n| Less: App commission (20%) | — | 12,00,000 | — |\n| Less: Delivery packaging (75,000 × ₹8) | — | 6,00,000 | — |\n| Less: Card and UPI charges (1%) | 80,000 | 60,000 | — |\n| Less: Freight (4,000 × ₹100) | — | — | 4,00,000 |\n| **Contribution** | **39,20,000** | **11,40,000** | **10,00,000** |\n| Less: Specific fixed costs | — | 3,00,000 | 4,80,000 |\n| **Contribution after specific fixed costs** | **39,20,000** | **8,40,000** | **5,20,000** |\n| Less: Common fixed costs (sales value, 4 : 3 : 2) | 5,60,000 | 4,20,000 | 2,80,000 |\n| **Profit** | **33,60,000** | **4,20,000** | **2,40,000** |\n\nTotal profit = ₹40,20,000 (contribution ₹60,60,000 − specific ₹7,80,000 − common ₹12,60,000).\n\n**(ii) Contribution ratios**\n\n| Measure | Cafés | App | Supermarkets |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Sales per order (₹) | 40 | 80 | 1,000 |\n| Contribution per ₹100 of sales (₹) | 49.00 | 19.00 | 25.00 |\n| Contribution per order (₹) | 19.60 | 15.20 | 250.00 |\n\n**(iii) Where to spend ₹5,00,000**\n\n| Channel | Extra contribution on ₹25,00,000 of sales (₹) | Less: advertising (₹) | Gain / (loss) (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Cafés (49%) | 12,25,000 | 5,00,000 | 7,25,000 |\n| App (19%) | 4,75,000 | 5,00,000 | (25,000) |\n| Supermarkets (25%) | 6,25,000 | 5,00,000 | 1,25,000 |\n\nSpend it on the cafés. Ananya's measure, sales per order, ignores the costs each channel's sales carry: the app's 20% commission and packaging leave only ₹19 of every ₹100 of app sales as contribution, the lowest of the three, and its extra sales would not even pay for the advertising. A channel is judged by contribution per rupee of sales (and its specific fixed costs), not by the size of its orders.\n::::\n:::::\n\n:::: example The kulhad unit's odd items: where does each go?\nTapri's kulhad unit made and sold 6,00,000 kulhads in 2025-26. Its records show direct materials ₹9,00,000, direct wages ₹6,00,000, factory overheads ₹4,80,000, administration overheads ₹2,40,000 and routine selling and distribution overheads ₹1,50,000. In addition:\n\n(a) A night shift allowance of ₹60,000, paid because Diwali demand exceeded the kiln's normal day capacity.\n\n(b) A night shift allowance of ₹24,000, paid only to finish a hotel chain's order of 40,000 kulhads on time.\n\n(c) ₹4,50,000 spent on developing a new glaze; production with it will be fully established next year, and 15,00,000 glazed kulhads are expected over three years.\n\n(d) ₹1,20,000 of research on a biodegradable lid that failed.\n\n(e) ₹90,000 on fancy printed sleeves to attract buyers.\n\n(f) ₹1,20,000 on cartons to carry kulhads safely from the unit to the cafés.\n\n(g) ₹80,000 for removing and re-erecting the kiln, which had been sited wrongly when the unit was planned.\n\n(h) ₹60,000 for training new potters, at the normal level for the unit.\n\n**Required:**\n\n(i) State the treatment of each item (a) to (h).\n\n(ii) Prepare a cost sheet for the year showing works cost, cost of production, cost of sales and cost per kulhad, with the hotel chain's order costed separately.\n\n::: answer Show the working\n**(i) Treatment of each item**\n\n| Item | Treatment (SM 4.60–4.63) |\n|---|---|\n| (a) Night shift, general pressure of work | Production overhead, recovered as such |\n| (b) Night shift for one customer's order | Charged directly to that order |\n| (c) Development of a new glaze | Deferred revenue expense; recovered from next year at ₹4,50,000 ÷ 15,00,000 = ₹0.30 a kulhad; nothing charged this year |\n| (d) Failed research | Excluded from costs; Costing Profit and Loss Account |\n| (e) Fancy sleeves to attract buyers | Advertising: selling overhead |\n| (f) Cartons to transport the product | Distribution overhead |\n| (g) Re-erection due to faulty planning | Abnormal: Costing Profit and Loss Account |\n| (h) Normal training of factory workers | Part of the cost of production (production overhead) |\n\n**(ii) Cost sheet for 2025-26 (6,00,000 kulhads)**\n\n| Particulars | Amount (₹) | Per kulhad (₹) |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Direct materials | 9,00,000 | 1.50 |\n| Direct wages | 6,00,000 | 1.00 |\n| **Prime cost** | **15,00,000** | **2.50** |\n| Factory overheads | 4,80,000 | |\n| Night shift allowance, general (a) | 60,000 | |\n| Training of potters (h) | 60,000 | |\n| **Works cost** | **21,00,000** | **3.50** |\n| Administration overheads | 2,40,000 | 0.40 |\n| **Cost of production** | **23,40,000** | **3.90** |\n| Selling and distribution: routine | 1,50,000 | |\n| Selling: fancy sleeves (e) | 90,000 | |\n| Distribution: transport cartons (f) | 1,20,000 | |\n| **Cost of sales** | **27,00,000** | **4.50** |\n\n**Hotel chain's order of 40,000 kulhads:** 40,000 × ₹4.50 = ₹1,80,000, plus its own night shift allowance (b) ₹24,000 = **₹2,04,000**, or ₹5.10 a kulhad. The other 5,60,000 kulhads cost ₹4.50 each.\n\nTotal cost for the year, including the hotel order's own charge, = ₹27,24,000. Kept out of the year's costs: failed research ₹1,20,000 and re-erection ₹80,000 (₹2,00,000 to Costing P&L), and the development cost ₹4,50,000 carried forward as deferred revenue expenditure.\n:::\n::::\n\n## Under the hood\n\n### Reverse questions are three equations in disguise\n\nEvery \"find the recovery rates\" question uses the same three identities: total cost from the selling price and the profit percentage; factory cost from total cost and the administration percentage; factory overheads as the gap between factory cost and prime cost. With one job and one unknown rate (PYQ January 2026 MCQ 12) it is two divisions and a subtraction; with two jobs and two unknown rates (Illustration 9), divide one job's equation by the other's.\n\n### Where these overheads sit in the cost sheet\n\nAdministration related to production is added before cost of production; general administration, selling and distribution after cost of goods sold, with interest and financing charges as a separate item of cost of sales ([[cma.sheet.mfg|cost sheet for a manufacturer]]). When they are absorbed at pre-determined rates, their over- or under-absorption is a reconciling item between cost and financial profit: the reconciliations in PYQ May 2024 and MTP January 2026 Series I both list administration overheads over-absorbed ([[cma.sys.recon|reconciling cost and financial profit]]).\n\n### Comparing absorption rates as control\n\nAdministration's first control method ends in a comparison of absorption rates and of over- or under-absorption from period to period (SM 4.48–4.49): the same tool that judges factory overheads ([[cma.oh.underover|under- and over-absorption]]).\n\n## Exam angle\n\n::: exam How ICAI asks these overheads\nIn the eight documents read for this lesson:\n\n- **Reverse calculation of recovery rates, 2–10 marks**: PYQ January 2026 MCQ 12 (one job, find the factory overhead percentage, 2 marks); old scheme May 2022 Q2(a) (two jobs, rates, statement, quote for a third job at a margin on selling price, 10 marks); old scheme MTP November 2023 Series II Q4(b) (Illustration 9's data).\n- **Last year's rates applied to an order, 6–8 marks**: PYQ January 2025 Q4(b) (production overhead on labour cost, marketing and distribution on production cost, price at 20% on sales, 6 marks); MTP May 2024 Series II Q4(a) (administration and selling and distribution as percentages of works cost, 8 marks); PYQ September 2026 Q2(b) (administration as a percentage of factory cost for a special order, 7 marks).\n- **Theory, 4–5 marks**: PYQ January 2025 Q6(c) (4 marks), \"Explain the methods that can be used for controlling Selling and Distribution Overheads\"; MTP September 2025 Series I Q6(d) (OR, 4 marks), \"Research and Development Expenses and its treatment in costing\"; old scheme MTP December 2021 Series I Q6(d) (5 marks), fringe benefits and bad debts. The chapter's own question 3 asks the problems of controlling selling and distribution overheads.\n- **Product-wise profitability with selling and distribution costs on drivers**: RTP January 2025 Q10 (packing per parcel, commission on sales, stationery per invoice, fixed costs apportioned; examine a recommendation to discontinue a product), the pattern of Illustration 10.\n:::\n\n::: exam Traps\n- \"Profit on total cost\" divides by (1 + p); \"profit on selling price\" multiplies by (1 − p), or divides the cost by (1 − p).\n- Read the base: administration on factory cost (works cost) is not administration on prime cost or on cost of production.\n- Special packing at a customer's request is charged to that order (SM 4.60). Order-specific costs given separately are added outside the percentages: ICAI's answer to PYQ January 2025 Q4(b) added the order's packaging and transportation after the percentage-based overheads, and PYQ September 2026 Q2(b) gives delivery and installation at the customer's site in the same way.\n- Bad debts: the chapter's own MCQ (SM 4.66, question 7) answers \"selling overhead\" (SM 4.80).\n- For S&D control ICAI's answer gives three methods, each with one line of how it works.\n:::\n\n::: trick P-C-F, top-down\nWork a reverse question from the top: Price → total Cost → Factory cost → factory overheads. Condition: the profit is stated on cost or on price, and each overhead on a single base.\n:::\n\n## Answer frame\n\n**\"Discuss the problems of controlling selling and distribution overheads\"** (SM question 3), then **\"methods\"** (PYQ January 2025 Q6(c), 4 marks):\n\n1. Problems: (i) external factors (distance of market, competition, terms of sale) beyond management's control; (ii) dependence on customers' behaviour and tastes, so control may lose customers; (iii) policy costs, not amenable to control.\n2. Methods: (a) comparison with past performance, also as a percentage of sales; suitable for small concerns; (b) budgetary control, fixed and variable, a flexible budget if needed, variances acted on; (c) standard costing, standards for salesmen, territories, products, variances enquired into.\n\n**\"Explain the methods of accounting administrative overheads\"**: the three methods in ICAI's order, each with its reason and its disadvantages (the table in How it works); for (c), the five bases.\n\n**\"Explain research and development expenses and their treatment in costing\"** (MTP September 2025, 4 marks): the two Terminology definitions; production-method research in cost of production; administration research in administration overhead; market research in selling and distribution; development of a particular product charged to it as deferred revenue expenditure, recovered per unit when production is fully established; routine general research to general overhead (deferred if substantial); fundamental research to administration; failed research to Costing P&L.\n\n**\"Treatment of bad debts in cost accounts\"**: no unanimity; view 1, excluded as financial losses; view 2, part of selling and distribution overheads in the normal course of trading; extraordinarily large bad debts excluded.\n\n### Three answers to the three-channels question\n\n::: cols\n**Loses marks**\n\n\"App orders are the largest consumer orders (₹80), so spend the ₹5,00,000 on the app.\"\n\nSales per order says nothing about the costs each rupee of sales carries.\n---\n**Passes**\n\nA profit statement with the common costs deducted before contribution, and the advice taken from the profit line.\n\nRight channel by luck; no contribution measure, no incremental working.\n---\n**Full marks**\n\nThe statement in layers (variable costs, contribution, specific fixed costs, apportioned common costs); contribution per ₹100 of sales and per order; the incremental table for the ₹5,00,000; a recommendation with its reason.\n:::\n\n## Common mistakes\n\n::: mistake Hall of Shame: the margin that moved\nTaking \"20% on selling price\" as 20% on cost prices a job costing ₹1,15,200 at ₹1,38,240 instead of ₹1,44,000. **Fix:** profit on price means cost is 80% of price: price = cost ÷ 0.80.\n:::\n\n::: mistake Administration on the wrong base\nApplying the 20% conversion-cost rate to the premix's works cost of ₹85 charges ₹17 of administration to a box instead of ₹5. **Fix:** apply each rate to the base it was computed on, and write the base next to every percentage in the statement, as ICAI does.\n:::\n\n::: mistake Dropping the product that \"makes a loss\"\nA product or channel shows a loss only after common fixed costs apportioned to it, which stay if it goes; in the channel example even the weakest channel earns ₹5,20,000 after its own fixed costs. **Fix:** look at contribution and at which fixed costs would really be saved.\n:::\n\n::: mistake Interest inside the factory\nAdding interest on borrowings to works cost or cost of production. **Fix:** interest and financing charges are a separate item of cost of sales (SM 4.60).\n:::\n\n## Practice\n\n::: quiz Check yourself\nQ: A factory absorbs administration overheads of ₹6,00,000 on conversion cost, which is budgeted at ₹30,00,000. A job has direct materials of ₹40,000, direct wages of ₹25,000 and factory overheads of ₹35,000. How much administration overhead is charged to the job (₹)?\n= 12000\n> Rate = ₹6,00,000 ÷ ₹30,00,000 = 20% of conversion cost. The job's conversion cost = ₹25,000 + ₹35,000 = ₹60,000, so administration = ₹12,000. Applying the 20% to the job's works cost (₹1,00,000) gives ₹20,000: a rate must be applied to the base it was computed on.\n\nQ: Case scenario. Tapri's lab spends ₹18 lakh this year developing a sugar-free chai premix, a single new product whose production will be fully established next year. Bunty, on an audit visit from Iyer & Associates, suggests charging it to the Costing Profit and Loss Account. Which treatment is correct?\n- [ ] Charge it to the Costing Profit and Loss Account this year\n- [ ] Add it to administration overheads\n- [x] Charge it to the premix as deferred revenue expenditure, recovered per unit once production is established\n- [ ] Add it to selling and distribution overheads\n> SM 4.63: development costs for a particular product are charged directly to that product, usually as deferred revenue expenses recovered as a cost per unit when production is fully established. Costing P&L is for research that fails.\n\nQ: Which basis does ICAI call ordinarily the most logical for absorbing selling overheads?\n- [ ] Cost of goods sold\n- [x] Sales value of goods\n- [ ] Gross profit on sales\n- [ ] Units produced\n> SM 4.53: sales value, because there is some connection between sales and the expenses incurred to achieve them. Cost of production may have no direct relationship; gross profit follows \"ability to pay\".\n\nQ: Why is control of selling and distribution overheads difficult? Select all that apply.\n- [x] Their incidence depends on external factors such as competition and distance of markets\n- [x] Controlling them may lose customers, whose tastes they depend on\n- [x] They are policy costs, not amenable to control\n- [ ] They are mostly variable with production volume\n> ICAI's three reasons (SM 4.55). They vary with sales and with policy, not with production volume.\n\nQ: How does ICAI treat a night shift allowance paid because work is beyond normal capacity, and one paid to finish a particular customer's order?\n- [ ] Both as direct wages of the jobs worked at night\n- [x] The first as production overhead; the second charged to that order\n- [ ] Both to the Costing Profit and Loss Account\n- [ ] The first to Costing P&L; the second as production overhead\n> SM 4.62: general pressure beyond normal capacity makes it a production overhead; a specific customer's order bears its own night shift cost; abnormal circumstances send it to Costing P&L.\n:::\n\n## Recap\n\n- Administration overhead (CIMA): costs of general management and secretarial, accounting and administrative services not directly related to production, marketing, research or development.\n- Three methods: apportion to production and sales; charge to Costing P&L; separate addition on works cost, sales, gross profit, quantity produced or conversion cost.\n- Control of administration: departmental analysis against results (and absorption rates), budgets, standards.\n- S&D bases: sales value (most logical), cost of goods sold, gross profit, orders or units. Best: estimated amount per unit (fixed by benefit + variable).\n- S&D control is hard (external factors, customers, policy costs); use past comparison, budgets, standard costing.\n- R&D: production methods, cost of production; administration or fundamental, administration overhead; market research, S&D; one product, deferred and charged to it; failure, Costing P&L.\n- Interest: separate item of cost of sales. Bad debts: two views; very large ones excluded. Carriage follows what it carries.\n- Reverse questions: Price → Cost → Factory cost → factory overheads. (The principal's salary still has to land somewhere.)"});
